# hcp::collector module Kconfig
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
menuconfig HCP_COLLECTOR
	bool "HCP collector (one queue, one worker: config, polling, reporting)"
	default n if BOARD_HCP_C5
	default y
	depends on HCP_MQTT && HCP_REGISTRY && HCP_STORAGE
	help
	  The business loop between Home Assistant and the Modbus bus: inbound topics
	  are routed onto one queue and acted on by one worker thread, which is the
	  only thread touching the bus and the point table. Replaces the old split
	  where a polling task published to MQTT inside the hardware mutex.

	  Off by default on the ESP32-C5 for now, and the reason is measured rather
	  than assumed: enabling it overflows that board's SRAM by 6,288 bytes at
	  link time. Its thread stack plus payload slots could be trimmed to about
	  half that, but the rest would have to come out of the network buffers or the
	  kernel thread pool on the one board nobody can re-test here (no C5 on the
	  bench). Cutting blind to make a linker happy is how a board gets a subtle
	  radio bug instead of an obvious build failure; it stays off until a C5 is
	  wired up and the numbers can be taken for real.

if HCP_COLLECTOR

config HCP_COLLECTOR_TICK_MS
	int "Worker wake-up interval (ms)"
	default 200
	help
	  How long the worker waits for a message before returning to its periodic
	  work (the poll schedule). Also the worst-case added latency of a command.

config HCP_COLLECTOR_QUEUE_DEPTH
	int "Queue depth (messages)"
	default 4 if BOARD_HCP_C5
	default 6

config HCP_COLLECTOR_SLOTS
	int "Payload buffers (one per in-flight message)"
	default 2 if BOARD_HCP_C5
	default 3

config HCP_COLLECTOR_MAX_MSG
	int "Largest payload kept (bytes)"
	default 512
	help
	  Matches the MQTT module's inbound limit; a config download with several
	  segments is a few hundred bytes of JSON.

config HCP_COLLECTOR_STACK_SIZE
	int "Worker stack size"
	default 2048 if BOARD_HCP_C5
	default 4096
	help
	  Sized for the deepest call on this thread (JSON parse, then a settings write
	  through hcp::storage). The ESP32-C5 variant is smaller because that board's
	  SRAM is the tightest of the three - its high-water mark has to be checked on
	  real hardware with the thread analyzer before it can be called comfortable.

endif
